{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC1 IS SETTLED BY THE CAPABILITY MAP: DC returns 2xx AND SILENTLY IGNORES\n\nCapability-map run **30863672922** (Jira DC 8.17.1, Server + Software licensed), raw REST with no\nrebar transport in the path:\n\n    PUT /rest/api/2/issue/SCRUM-6 {\"fields\":{\"parent\":{\"key\":\"SCRUM-1\"}}}  -> HTTP 204   [req-0056]\n    GET read-back: SCRUM-6 is STILL parented to SCRUM-5, not SCRUM-1               [req-0058]\n\n**The answer to AC1 is accept-and-ignore, not rejection.** A sub-task reparent reports success and\ndoes nothing. That is the dangerous branch of the two this ticket posed, and it confirms the reading\nI argued for over the inbound cell's own \"the write succeeded, the index is just lagging\"\nself-diagnosis — a search-index lag cannot make a direct field read return the old value.\n\nNote the payload shape matters: `{\"fields\":{\"parent\":null}}` returns **400** `data was not an\nobject` [req-0055], while the SET form returns 204-and-ignores. Same field, opposite failure modes.\n\n### What this means for the product\n\n`set_parent`'s sub-task branch writes `fields.parent` and gets a 204. Nothing downstream can tell\nthat the write did nothing — which is why this surfaced only as an unchanged field in a live oracle.\nAny fix must **verify by read-back**, because the status code is a lie here. Declining the write\n(as the epic branch does when the field is undiscoverable) is the other honest option.\n\nEvidence ids are in the run's `evidence.json`; the raw request/response for each is recorded there."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785803171905831001,"uuid":"e20b0d3b-483d-4e9d-8ec0-80d99942a9a5"}